{"database":{"advisory-count":1352,"last-commit":null,"last-updated":null},"lockfile":{"dependency-count":509},"settings":{"target_arch":[],"target_os":[],"severity":null,"ignore":[],"informational_warnings":["unmaintained","unsound","notice"]},"vulnerabilities":{"found":false,"count":0,"list":[]},"warnings":{"unmaintained":[{"kind":"unmaintained","package":{"name":"proc-macro-error","version":"1.0.4","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"da25490ff9892aab3fcf7c36f08cfb902dd3e71ca0f9f9517bea02a73a5ce38c","dependencies":[{"name":"proc-macro-error-attr","version":"1.0.4","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"proc-macro2","version":"1.0.107","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"quote","version":"1.0.47","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"syn","version":"1.0.109","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"version_check","version":"0.9.5","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null},"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2024-0370","package":"proc-macro-error","title":"proc-macro-error is unmaintained","description":"proc-macro-error's maintainer seems to be unreachable, with no commits for 2 years, no releases pushed for 4 years, and no activity on the GitLab repo or response to email.\n\nproc-macro-error also depends on `syn 1.x`, which may be bringing duplicate dependencies into dependant build trees.\n\n## Possible Alternative(s)\n\n- [manyhow](https://crates.io/crates/manyhow)\n- [proc-macro2-diagnostics](https://github.com/SergioBenitez/proc-macro2-diagnostics)","date":"2024-09-01","aliases":[],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":[],"keywords":[],"cvss":null,"informational":"unmaintained","references":[],"source":null,"url":"https://gitlab.com/CreepySkeleton/proc-macro-error/-/issues/20","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"affected":null,"versions":{"patched":[],"unaffected":[]}},{"kind":"unmaintained","package":{"name":"rustls-pemfile","version":"1.0.4","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"1c74cae0a4cf6ccbbf5f359f08efdf8ee7e1dc532573bf0db71968cb56b1448c","dependencies":[{"name":"base64","version":"0.21.7","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null},"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2025-0134","package":"rustls-pemfile","title":"rustls-pemfile is unmaintained","description":"The rustls-pemfile crate is no longer maintained. The repository has been archived since August\n2025, and users are encouraged to depend directly on the underlying PEM parsing code included\nin rustls-pki-types since 1.9.0. The latest version of rustls-pemfile is in fact a thin wrapper\naround the same code used in rustls-pki-types, so migrating should be straightforward.\n\nThe new API is represented by the [`PemObject`][PemObject] trait, which provides methods for\nreading a single or multiple PEM objects from a file or byte slice.\n\n[PemObject]: https://docs.rs/rustls-pki-types/latest/rustls_pki_types/pem/trait.PemObject.html","date":"2025-11-28","aliases":[],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":[],"keywords":[],"cvss":null,"informational":"unmaintained","references":[],"source":null,"url":"https://github.com/rustls/pemfile/issues/61","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"affected":null,"versions":{"patched":[],"unaffected":[]}}],"unsound":[{"kind":"unsound","package":{"name":"glib","version":"0.18.5","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"233daaf6e83ae6a12a52055f568f9d7cf4671dabb78ff9560ab6da230ce00ee5","dependencies":[{"name":"bitflags","version":"2.13.2","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"futures-channel","version":"0.3.34","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"futures-core","version":"0.3.34","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"futures-executor","version":"0.3.34","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"futures-task","version":"0.3.34","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"futures-util","version":"0.3.34","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"gio-sys","version":"0.18.1","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"glib-macros","version":"0.18.5","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"glib-sys","version":"0.18.1","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"gobject-sys","version":"0.18.0","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"libc","version":"0.2.191","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"memchr","version":"2.8.3","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"once_cell","version":"1.21.4","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"smallvec","version":"1.16.3","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"thiserror","version":"1.0.69","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null},"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2024-0429","package":"glib","title":"Unsoundness in `Iterator` and `DoubleEndedIterator` impls for `glib::VariantStrIter`","description":"The `VariantStrIter::impl_get` function (called internally by implementations of the `Iterator` and `DoubleEndedIterator` traits for this type) was unsound, resulting in undefined behaviour.\n\nAn immutable reference `&p` to a `*mut libc::c_char` pointer initialized to `NULL` was passed as an argument to a C function that that mutates the pointer behind `&p` in-place (i.e. as an out-argument), which was unsound. After changes in recent versions of the Rust compiler, these unsound writes through `&p` now seem to be completely disregarded when building the `glib` crate with optimizations.\n\nThis subsequently caused all calls of `VariantStrIter::impl_get` to violate the safety requirements of the `std::ffi::CStr::from_ptr` function - which requires its argument to be a valid pointer to a C-style string - resulting in crashes due to `NULL` pointer dereferences.\n\nThis was fixed by passing the out-argument pointer explitly as `&mut p` instead of `&p`.\n\nThis issue has been present since this code was initially added in `glib` v0.15.0. The mismatch in mutability was likely missed (and not raised as an error by the compiler) because the C function wrapped by `VariantStrIter::impl_get` is variadic (`glib_sys::g_variant_get_child`), and the pointer in question is one of the variadic arguments.","date":"2024-03-30","aliases":["GHSA-wrw7-89jp-8q8g"],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":[],"keywords":[],"cvss":null,"informational":"unsound","references":[],"source":null,"url":"https://github.com/gtk-rs/gtk-rs-core/pull/1343","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"affected":{"arch":[],"os":[],"functions":{"glib::VariantStrIter::last":[">=0.15.0, <0.20.0"],"glib::VariantStrIter::next":[">=0.15.0, <0.20.0"],"glib::VariantStrIter::next_back":[">=0.15.0, <0.20.0"],"glib::VariantStrIter::nth":[">=0.15.0, <0.20.0"],"glib::VariantStrIter::nth_back":[">=0.15.0, <0.20.0"]}},"versions":{"patched":[">=0.20.0"],"unaffected":["<0.15.0"]}}]}}
